Implement a new “MIM DB Workbench” with an AI-first MongoDB Query experience.

First inspect the existing Angular/Node architecture, MongoDB connection/configuration, user profiles and mim-expert/Kiro integration. Reuse existing services and connection handling; do not create duplicate Mongo auth/config systems.

GOAL:
Allow Developer/Tester users to query MongoDB in plain English without knowing Mongo syntax.

Examples:
- “Show me the latest record for PIN 123456”
- “Show last 10 records for this PIN”
- “Show failed records created today”
- “Compare the latest two records”
- “Show only records where status is PROCESSING”

1. DB Workbench Page

Create a dedicated DB Workbench page with:

- Database/Collection explorer
- AI Query input as the PRIMARY interface
- Results area with:
  Table | JSON | Tree | Visualize
- Generated Query expandable section
- execution time/result count
- query history
- saved/favorite queries
- “Ask MIM Expert about Results”

Keep an optional “Advanced Query” tab for developers who want to enter raw read-only Mongo filters/aggregations.

2. AI Query Flow

Flow:

Natural-language request
→ use selected DB/collection + discovered schema as context
→ generate a structured Mongo read query
→ validate it server-side
→ execute through Node/MongoDB integration
→ return formatted results.

Do NOT execute arbitrary AI-generated text directly.

Use a structured internal query model such as:
operation
database
collection
filter
projection
sort
limit
pipeline

The backend must convert this validated model into Mongo operations.

3. Context Awareness

The AI Query Builder must automatically know:
- selected database
- selected collection
- known fields/schema
- previous query/result context in the current DB session.

Support follow-ups such as:

“Show last 10 records for PIN 123”
→ “Only failed ones”
→ “Compare the latest two”
→ “Show status history”

Follow-ups must reuse previous DB/query context.

If no collection is selected, use available schema/project metadata to infer the most relevant collection when confidence is sufficient.

If the request cannot be mapped safely, do not run a query; return a clear blocker.

4. READ-ONLY SAFETY

DB Workbench must be strictly read-only, especially for PROD.

Allow only operations such as:
- find
- aggregate
- count
- distinct
- sort
- projection
- limit
- explain
- read-only schema/index metadata

Hard-block both UI and backend execution of:
- insert
- update
- delete
- replace
- drop
- createIndex
- $out
- $merge
- any write/admin operation.

Do not rely only on hidden UI controls; enforce this in backend validation.

Add safe limits:
- configurable maximum rows
- query timeout
- aggregation limits where appropriate.

Show a visible:
PROD • READ ONLY
badge when connected to production.

5. Generated Query

After natural-language interpretation, show the actual generated Mongo query in an expandable “Generated Query” section.

Example:

User:
“Give me last 10 records for PIN 123456”

Generated query:
find({ pin: "123456" })
.sort({ createdAt: -1 })
.limit(10)

The query preview is for transparency/debugging; execution must still use the validated structured query model.

6. Result Experience

Make results highly readable.

Provide:

Table View
→ sortable columns, nested-field support, pagination.

JSON View
→ formatted/collapsible JSON.

Tree View
→ expandable nested object/array hierarchy.

Visualize
→ intelligently suggest useful charts based on result field types using the existing/new visualization framework.

Examples:
status distribution → pie/bar
timestamps → timeline
numeric values → line/bar distribution.

Never force visualization when data is not suitable.

7. Ask MIM Expert

Allow:
- entire result set
- selected rows/documents
- selected fields

to be passed to:
“Ask MIM Expert”

Automatically provide context:
database
collection
query
selected records
field names

Then mim-expert may use DB + GitLab + Splunk + OpenShift + KB only as needed to answer questions such as:

“Why is this record still PROCESSING?”
“Which code writes this field?”
“Find logs related to this transaction.”

Do not automatically send huge raw result sets; pass only relevant selected/contextual data.

8. Profile Awareness

Reuse the existing Response Profile.

Developer:
show AI Query + Advanced Query + raw JSON + schema/index/explain details.

Tester:
prioritize AI Query, filters, readable table/tree results, comparison and saved queries.

Business:
hide DB Workbench by default unless existing permissions/product rules explicitly allow it.

Profile affects UX, not DB safety.

9. Architecture

Interactive DB queries must NOT depend on Kiro/MCP for every database call.

Use:

Angular DB Workbench
→ Node backend
→ existing MongoDB driver/connection
→ MongoDB

Use mim-expert/Kiro only for:
- natural-language interpretation/query generation
- Ask MIM Expert reasoning.

Keep Mongo authentication/connection credentials backend-only and never expose them to Angular, prompts, logs or console output.

10. UX

When Run is clicked:
→ immediately show Generating Query
→ Validating
→ Executing
→ Results

Never leave the UI appearing idle while work is happening.

Handle clearly:
connection failure
invalid query
collection not found
timeout
zero results
AI interpretation failure.

Preserve all existing app functionality.

Target experience:

User selects collection
→ asks “Give me the latest data for PIN 12345”
→ AI creates a safe read-only query
→ backend validates and executes it
→ user gets readable Table/JSON/Tree/Visual results
→ user can continue with follow-up queries or Ask MIM Expert about the data.



