Enhance GitLab setup and repository cloning support.

First inspect the existing Configuration, GitLab integration, Manage Project flow and Git actions. Reuse existing services/state; do not create duplicate auth systems.

1. GitLab Configuration
Keep the existing PAT field and Connect & Test flow.

Add a separate SSH section for Git operations:

GitLab API
- Personal Access Token
- existing Connect & Test

Git over SSH
- automatically detect existing system SSH configuration/ssh-agent first
- optional SSH private-key path field only if explicit path selection is needed
- add “Test SSH Connection”

Never store or display private-key contents.

Show separate health states:
- GitLab API: Connected / Error
- Git over SSH: Connected / Error / Not Configured

PAT and SSH serve different purposes:
PAT → GitLab API/repository metadata/code access for mim-expert.
SSH → clone/fetch/pull/push and normal Git transport.

2. SSH Validation
“Test SSH Connection” must verify real GitLab SSH authentication using the configured GitLab host.

Do not modify keys or GitLab account configuration automatically.

If auth fails, show a clear actionable error without exposing sensitive data.

3. Clone Missing Repository
On Manage Project, when the globally selected repo does not exist under the configured Project Root, do not show an empty/broken page.

Show:
“Repository is not available locally”
[ Clone Repository ]

On Clone Repository:
- obtain the selected project’s SSH clone URL from the existing GitLab integration
- verify destination path is safe
- verify SSH authentication
- clone into `<ProjectRoot>/<repo>`
- use the repository’s default branch
- show immediate progress using the existing console/progress/toastr framework
- after success, refresh only that repository and open the normal Manage Project view.

4. Safety
- Clone only on explicit user action.
- Never overwrite/delete an existing directory.
- If same-name directory exists but is not a valid matching Git repo, stop with Needs Attention.
- Never expose PAT, private-key data or secrets in command output/logs/UI.
- Do not silently fall back to insecure credential handling.
- Preserve all existing Git safeguards and functionality.

Expected setup:

PAT test ✓
SSH test ✓
Project Root configured
→ user selects repo
→ local repo missing
→ Clone Repository
→ SSH clone completes
→ repo becomes immediately available in Manage Project/Web IDE.



